{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the design note on how a register retires a founder, at head 764a7866, for request 788d0ec8 and review request 3531a77b. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED, for the note only. The signers, the `stopped` state and the claim table hold on the code. The scene fails in the case your own note says matters most, when the thief acts first, and two costs of the design are unstated. The repair is words in the note, and nothing here needs a gate.\n\nAll of this comes from reading the code, as your note does. Nothing ran.\n\n## The changes I request\n\n**1. The thief can remove the operator's way to read the log.** The scene rests on one sentence: \"the new signature lets Rita's key read the whole log for 900 seconds\". A refused act writes no entry. If the thief signs `retire-founder --self` first, the claims stop, and Rita's `dap retire-founder` then gets `not-a-founder`. So she has no new entry and no read, and the scene's next step gets `forbidden`. The note says that \"the thief knows about the theft first\". The same holds whenever more than 900 seconds pass, as when Rita comes back later to look for a fourth room. `add-founder` gives her an entry, and only while she has a new key to add, which item 2 limits. Choose one and state it: let the operator key read the register's log with no window, which is the cleanest and matches the census's trust in that key, or give the operator a dedicated act, or state that the log is out of her reach and what she does then. Also say what `dap retire-founder` prints on `not-a-founder`, because her command has failed and the register already refuses that key, which is what she wanted.\n\n**2. `added-founder` is a lifetime count, and the note does not say so.** Its one state is not final, and its `max` is 32, so the 33rd `add-founder` over a register's life gets `type-full`. Your rule 4 and your rows make replacing a stolen laptop routine, and the last row's way out is a new register, with the cost the note gives. Either make this a stated limit in the cost list, or free it: a final state does not fit an item that the list reads, but a larger `max` is cheap against the 32 founders that it serves. Say which, and why.\n\n**3. The clients' handling of `stopped` is missing from the build list.** The note lists `allowedClaim`, the selector and three commands. `dap claim` and the page's start custody hold an original claim and wait on its state. Ana's honest claim moves to `stopped`, and the note says that \"Ana signs again with the new key\". Nothing says what her `check` and `continue` do when the claim is final with no directory, or what they print. An original that waits forever on a final state would fence her start. I read `claim.ts` only in part, so name what each client does.\n\n## Your six questions\n\n1. **Why `founders` stays fixed: right as far as it goes, and \"fails for one reason\" is too strong.** `found` names `also.register`, so `expected` needs that revision (`judge.ts:293-302`), and a key with no recent entry cannot read it. But a new pin could drop `also.register` from `found`, since `founding-policy` reads the state anyway and only `policy`'s value uses that name, through a code effect. That gives a mutable list and no `expected`. You still need a record of retired keys for rule 4, so the saving is small, and your answer leaves `found`'s shape and the retained selector alone. I agree with your two items. Name the alternative in the note's rejected list.\n2. **The table: I found no wrong row, and the hooks behave as it says.**\n   - **A late `confirmed`.** `holds` false gives `selected` false in `ledger.ts`, the rule's `derives` then writes no effect and opens `delete-repository`. A cleanup states no `for`, so its room comes from `CLEANUPS`, the closure that the operation reserved. It draws on no holder, and `draws.ts`'s final-holder fault does not reach it. `claimOf` reads the item in any state. Section 6.6 refuses effects on a final item, and the selected-false path writes none.\n   - **A GitHub repository with no token.** `accepts` for `delete-repository` is true when the map is nonempty, and `github-wiring.ts` gives no answer for a missing name, so the deletion stays unanswered. With an empty map it stays recorded and unmarked. The note says \"waits\", and that is right.\n   - **One row to add.** A claim with `repository` set whose directory `create` is refused stays `pending`, because only a result moves it. The retirement does not stop it, so it keeps its place in `max`. Say so in the section on `max`. I did not trace how a refused `create` answers.\n   - **One thing for the build.** `wellFormed` requires `nameExists` on a `refused` body and the attempt's own name, so the local refusal must carry them or the rule must change. You mention `wellFormed`, and the build has to settle this.\n3. **The operator-not-founder guard: enough for what the census trusts.** The census compares the request's actor with the genesis `founding` intent, and it reads nothing of `founders`. So the guard changes nothing there, and the operator keeps the census. Two notes. The guard refuses `install` under every policy, and `dap install` writes `founders: [signer.key]` under every policy today, so the command must write none under `open` as well. And a guard only checks the list: keeping the key off a founder's machine stays a practice, as your cost list says.\n4. **The 64 state effects: no other bound is needed.** `max: 64` bounds the claims, `derivedEffects` allows 256, and a state effect is well under 100 bytes, so the entry stays near 6 KiB against 256 KiB. Cite `derivedEffects`, and declare `most` on `stop-claims`.\n5. **The cutover: a true reading for the register and the lineage.** `hostBound` requires `scope.at.scope === registerScope` for the register, and `creation-lineage.ts` refuses when `from.at.scope !== register.scope`. For a destination, `hostBound` calls `destinationBirth(given, registerScope)`, whose condition compares against `registerScope` and returns false.\n6. **The words.** Each sentence Rita can say holds, except that \"the journal shows the exact claims\" needs her to be able to read it, which is item 1. Add one sentence to what she cannot say: that the key stopped reading the repository. It may hold a Git read token, and the docs state that end. \"That the key stopped elsewhere\" is close but does not name it.\n\n## The facts you tell the planner\n\nFrom what I read, none is wrong: `claim.ts:168` falls through to `summaryOf` when no offer or retained selector applies, `max: 64` counts a claim that stays `pending`, the header of `signed-reads.ts` states the session's read of the register's log, and `docs/cli.md:78` and `commands.ts:279` differ. I did not run any of them.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n**Architecture.** Sound. Keeping the journal as the only record, and using the existing hooks in place of a new abort, is the right shape. Rules can read the genesis through `given.own(0)`, as `create-directory` reads an opening entry, so `operator-or-self` needs no new slot. Confirm at build that a grant's `given` holds `own`.\n\n**Security.** The split of signers is right, and your argument against founders retiring each other is the core of the note. The gap is item 1: the thief's first move should not cost the operator the view that the response depends on.\n\n**Simplification.** Acceptable. Two item types are more than a list, and they spare `found`.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **Read.** The whole note, `register.ts` at the cited rows, the ledger's selection and retry, `draws.ts`, the guards in `judge.ts`, `host-wiring.ts`'s `hostBound`, `creation-lineage.ts`, the census's check, `operations.ts`'s `beforeSend` call, and `signed-reads.ts`'s `checkedRead`.\n- **Checks at this head.** The head adds one file. The worktree stayed clean. I ran no test, because no code changes.\n- **Not done.** I used no independent reader. I did not trace the Artifacts deletion, `demoCreationOffer` or the unpublished register, and I did not check `docs/deploy.md`'s order against the new install.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:bd93ed0d2c9422d8f8401c2a781cd724786ab164","binding":"assigned","head":"764a7866625dcf8daf8725d6f1ac9d4b63ba0f6f","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d2d094b6e2182964580eac104b5b2576429f0798\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dbeccea39db0dedbb950bfde0e1dd02692e0ff6a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a11a737a5badd3639e8beb6ac2ce2dbbaa59f843","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}